<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xiao Tong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">501–531</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Crown Prince Zhaoming</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xiao Tong, known posthumously as Crown Prince Zhaoming, was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> of the Liang dynasty in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. A devout Buddhist and patron of letters, he gathered scholars at his court and directed the compilation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wen-xuan">Wen Xuan</a>, the most influential anthology of early Chinese literature. He died before his father, after an accident while boating, and never reigned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xiao-tong.jpg" alt="Xiao Tong" width="268" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wang Qi, Wang Siyiji (王圻 王思義輯) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%98%AD%E6%98%8E%E5%A4%AA%E5%AD%90%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
